# Rehearsal Mixes

Practice audio is built by the CLI's `rehearsal-mixes` command. It exports
one audio stem per staff from Sibelius, then mixes them with ffmpeg. The
score is not changed.

## Before exporting

1. **ffmpeg** must be on `PATH`, and the bridge must be v55 or newer.
2. **Check the sounds.** Run `playback-sounds` to see each staff's sound
   set, patch and any Mixer override. If the user wants a specific library
   (for example Cantai), pass `--require-sound <name>` to the export so it
   refuses to run if any voice plays something else.
3. **One note per staff.** If a voice staff has chords or divisi, the
   rendered sound may drop notes. Offer the `playback-divisi-split` skill
   first to build a playback copy.
4. **Staff names.** Voices are the staves with lyrics; file names come from
   staff names. Name unnamed staves with `--name 1=Soprano` (repeatable).
   For an instrumental score, `--all-parts` makes every staff a part.
5. **One score open.** `--file` does not bring a score that is already open
   to the front, so a batch can silently export the wrong score. Ask the user
   to close other scores, and always pass the global `--expect-file SCORE`
   so a mismatch stops the export.

## Cantai

Cantai is set up by the user in Sibelius, not by the CLI:

- **Assign voices in the Mixer.** The playback configuration alone leaves
  singers on the default sounds; the user picks a Cantai voice on each
  staff's Mixer strip. `playback-sounds` then shows `Cantai / ...` with
  `[Mixer override ...]`. Save the score so the choice is kept.
- **One language at a time.** Cantai sings one language (English, Latin or
  Spanish) and re-renders every voice when it changes. Group pieces by
  language and let the user switch between groups; export nothing while it
  re-renders.
- **Give every staff a different sound.** Two staves with the same sound
  share one playback channel, and Cantai then sings only one of them live,
  for example Soprano I and II. Use a different Cantai voice for each
  (Soprano and "Ottetto: Sopranos"), and check the channel numbers differ.
- **Keep instrument changes to a minimum.** Repeated `set-initial-instrument`
  calls on a Cantai score left one staff rendering an instrument sound even
  in export. A fresh import with voices set once in the Mixer fixed it.
- `--require-sound Cantai` only checks the sound set name, not that each
  staff has the right voice: read the `playback-sounds` list. `override` can
  be empty while `actualSoundId` is still a Cantai voice (the export passes);
  read `actualSoundId` to see which voice will sing.
- **Cantai ignores Sibelius's rhythmic feel.** Swing set under Play /
  Performance / Rhythmic Feel (or as "Swing" text) is not heard in Cantai
  exports. Use the `swing-playback-copy` skill to build a swung copy.

## Export

```bash
uv run --project "${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}" sibelius-cli playback-sounds
uv run --project "${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}" sibelius-cli --expect-file SCORE rehearsal-mixes --out DIR \
  [--file SCORE] [--name 1=Soprano] [--all-parts] [--require-sound Cantai] \
  [--bitrate 192k] [--loudness -16] [--keep-stems]
```

Allow about 10 seconds per staff: each staff is exported twice, because an
export's opening can carry the previous staff's voice. That does not cure a
stale render cache (see Afterwards). Read
`--help` before composing unusual invocations. Quote `--name` values that
contain spaces (`--name "2=Contra I"`). Importing MusicXML shows an options
dialog every time; the user must click OK before the import continues.

## Single-staff melody line

A one-staff score (a melody with lyrics) still makes the three files, and
they are identical because there is no accompaniment or second voice. Pass
`--name 1=Melody`, keep the "All voices" file, rename it `<Song> - Melody.mp3`
(`<Song> - Melody (swing).mp3` for a swing copy) and delete the other two.
When the user wants to listen before each export, do one score per request:
confirm `score-snapshot` shows the right file, `playback-sounds` shows Cantai,
then export with `--expect-file`.

## Output

- `<score> - All voices.mp3`
- `<voice> only.mp3`: that voice plus accompaniment
- `<voice> louder.mp3`: that voice at 3x amplitude over the others

Voices are first equalized to one loudness; accompaniment keeps its
Sibelius level. Each file is then gained as a whole to `--loudness`
(default -16 LUFS), lowered if it would peak above -1 dBFS, so every file
plays at the same level. `--keep-stems` keeps the per-staff stems.

## Afterwards

The command proof-listens to the mixes itself (see the `proof-listen-mixes`
skill) and exits non-zero if a check fails: do not upload mixes that fail.
A `shared` error or a loud opening means Cantai served a stale render for the
first bars; restart Sibelius and export again.

Still listen to the start of at least one "only" file to confirm no other
voice bleeds in, and report the files written to the user.
